---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import VdevLab from "../../components/VdevLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D25.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("vdevs");
const VI = "include/sys/vdev_impl.h";
const VM = "module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c";
const Z = "module/zfs/zio.c";
const SM = "module/zfs/spa_misc.c";
const SP = "module/zfs/spa.c";
const V = "module/zfs/vdev.c";
const DR = "module/zfs/vdev_draid.c";
const L = {
  ioStart: gh(Z, 4831),
  rootOps: gh("module/zfs/vdev_root.c", 154, 155),
  skipMissing: gh(VM, 554, 559),
  load: gh(VM, 567, 586),
  childIo: gh(VM, 689, 692),
  hasDdt: gh(SM, 2111, 2115),
  metadataType: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 116),
  smallblk: gh("module/zcommon/zfs_prop.c", 739, 742),
  reserve: gh(SM, 443, 448),
  zilOrder: gh(Z, 4561, 4595),
  again: gh(Z, 4430, 4444),
  why: gh("module/zfs/metaslab.c", 5401, 5410),
  copies: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 2400, 2404),
  redundant: gh("module/zcommon/zfs_prop.c", 412, 415),
  rotor: gh("module/zfs/metaslab.c", 639, 698),
  mirrorOps: gh(VM, 971, 995),
  spareOps: gh(VM, 1023, 1047),
  newId: gh(V, 1426),
  attachLast: gh(SP, 8449, 8454),
  dtl: gh(SP, 8499, 8500),
  topTransfer: gh(V, 1299, 1330),
  removeParent: gh(V, 1455, 1504),
  unspare: gh(SP, 9462, 9480),
  derived: gh(DR, 2291, 2294),
  rowHeight: gh("include/sys/vdev_draid.h", 45, 48),
  getPerm: gh(DR, 560, 576),
  maps: gh(DR, 171, 176),
  verify: gh(DR, 452, 460),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="vdevs"
  title="Where does ZFS put a block in my pool? The vdevs, allocation classes and disk replacement · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS picks a class of vdevs for each block by its kind, then sends the write down a tree where every node passes it on its own way; a disk being replaced is a mirror for a while. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>Where does ZFS put a block in my pool?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          A pool is built from vdevs, short for virtual devices: mirrors, RAID-Z groups, dRAID groups or single disks.
          Beside the vdevs that hold your data, a pool can have a log device for synchronous writes, a special device for
          metadata and a dedup device for the deduplication table. Any disk can be replaced while the pool runs. For each
          block it writes, ZFS has to choose one of these vdevs, then get the block onto the disks under it, whatever the
          layout looks like that day.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS first chooses a class from the kind of block. The vdevs that hold data form the normal class, and each kind
          of special-purpose device forms a class of its own. Metadata goes to the special device when the pool has one.
          File data goes there only if the block is no larger than a size you set per dataset, zero by default, and only
          while the special device is less than three-quarters full. The deduplication table goes to the dedup device, the
          intent log (the ZIL) to the log device or, without one, to space set aside on the other vdevs, and everything
          else to the normal class. When a class is full, ZFS chooses again without it. Within the class, the allocator
          spreads blocks over the vdevs by free space and load. Metadata is stored twice by default, and the second copy
          goes on the next vdev of the class.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the write goes down a tree. The pool is the root, the vdevs are its children and the disks are theirs. Each
          node has its own small table of functions that says how an I/O passes on to its children: a mirror sends a write
          to all its disks and a read to one, and a RAID-Z group cuts the block into columns, one per disk. Replacing a disk
          adds a node to that tree. The old disk and the new one become the two sides of a temporary mirror, so writes
          reach both, and reads skip the new disk for blocks it does not have yet. When the resilver, the copy onto the new
          disk, is done, the old disk and the temporary node go away, and no other part of ZFS had to know.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pool">
      <h2 id="pool"><span class="n">1</span> Build a pool and write blocks</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The tree is a pool with three vdevs for data (a mirror, a RAID-Z group of three disks and a dRAID of five) and,
          to start with, a log mirror and a special mirror. Write each kind of block and see where it lands. The line
          under the buttons says where it went, the vdevs that got a copy are ringed, and its class is marked in the
          table. Then take the special mirror out and write metadata again, set <code>special_small_blocks</code> to
          16 KiB and write a small file block, or fill the special class and see the block go to the normal class.
        </p>
        <p>
          The last block panel lists each step of the choice and the path of the write down the tree. Select a node to
          see its table of functions. A mirror writes to every child and reads from one. A RAID-Z or dRAID group writes
          one column per child. The dRAID spreads its groups over all five children through a fixed table of
          permutations, so two large blocks in a row land on different sets of disks.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then select a disk and replace it. It becomes the first child of a replacing node, with the new disk beside it.
          Read the last block back to see the new disk skipped, then press Resilver done. A hot spare makes a spare node
          instead, and that node stays after the resilver, until the original disk is back or one of the two is detached.
          On a dRAID child you can use the distributed spare, which is space set aside on every child of the dRAID.
        </p>
      </div>
      <VdevLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows nine places in the source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>One table of functions per node</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every node of the tree is a <code>vdev_t</code>, and its type is nothing but the table of functions it points
          to, a <code>vdev_ops_t</code>. The I/O path does not look at the type. It calls the node's
          <code>vdev_op_io_start</code> and lets it decide how the I/O goes on.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_ops_t</code>, vdev_impl.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VI}
            from={104}
            to={113}
            symbol="vdev_ops_t"
            notes={[
              { from: 104, to: 105, text: "Open and close the device. A mirror's open opens every child." },
              { from: 106, to: 109, text: "Size translation: a raidz turns a block's size into a larger allocated size, to leave room for parity." },
              {
                from: 110,
                to: 111,
                text: `How an I/O goes on to the children, and how their results come back. The I/O path only <a href="${L.ioStart}">calls this pointer</a>. The <a href="${L.rootOps}">root has neither</a>: an I/O never starts at the root.`,
              },
              { from: 113, text: "Whether a block touches a child that missed writes. A resilver asks this before reading the block." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A mirror: one child for a read, all of them for a write</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_mirror_io_start</code> is the mirror's function. A normal read picks one child and a write takes them
          all. The replacing and spare nodes use this same function, which is why a replacement needs no code of its own
          in the I/O path.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_mirror_io_start</code>, vdev_mirror.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VM}
            from={656}
            to={669}
            symbol="vdev_mirror_io_start"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 656,
                to: 660,
                text: `A normal read picks one child: not one that is <a href="${L.skipMissing}">missing the block's txg</a>, and among the others <a href="${L.load}">the least loaded</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 662,
                to: 668,
                text: `A write goes to every child. The loop that follows issues <a href="${L.childIo}">one child I/O per child</a>, at the same offset and size.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Choosing a class</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>spa_preferred_class</code> chooses the class of every block except the ZIL's. It looks at the kind of
          block, at which classes the pool has, and at the class it may already have tried.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>spa_preferred_class</code>, spa_misc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SM}
            from={2149}
            to={2178}
            symbol="spa_preferred_class"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2149,
                to: 2156,
                text: `A block of the deduplication table goes to dedup if the pool has a dedup vdev, else to special if the pool <a href="${L.hasDdt}">keeps the table there</a>, else to normal. A class already tried is skipped.`,
              },
              { from: 2158, to: 2159, text: "No special vdev, or special already tried: normal." },
              {
                from: 2161,
                to: 2163,
                text: `Metadata (an object type marked so, <a href="${L.metadataType}">dnodes</a> among them) and the indirect blocks of files: special.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2169,
                to: 2176,
                text: `A block no larger than <code>special_small_blocks</code> (<a href="${L.smallblk}">0 by default</a>) goes to special while special has allocated less than 75% of its space. The <a href="${L.reserve}">last 25%</a> is kept for metadata.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2178,
                text: `Everything else: normal. A ZIL block never comes here: <code>zio_alloc_zil</code> tries <a href="${L.zilOrder}">its own order</a>, log, special's embedded log, special, the embedded log of normal vdevs, then normal.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>When a class is full</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When the chosen class has no room left, <code>zio_dva_allocate</code> asks for a class again. The second answer
          is different, because the class that failed is now marked as tried.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_dva_allocate</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={4404}
            to={4407}
            symbol="zio_dva_allocate"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4407,
                text: `On <code>ENOSPC</code>, <code>spa_preferred_class</code> is called again and returns the next class. The I/O records it and <a href="${L.again}">starts the allocation over</a> there.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The next copy on the next vdev</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A block with several copies has one address per copy, a DVA. <code>metaslab_alloc_dva_range</code> puts each copy
          after the first on the vdev that follows the previous copy's vdev in the class.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_alloc_dva_range</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/metaslab.c"
            from={5420}
            to={5427}
            symbol="metaslab_alloc_dva_range"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 5420,
                to: 5423,
                text: `Copy <code>d</code> starts at the group after the one of copy <code>d - 1</code>: the next vdev of the class, round the class. The comment above gives the reason: <a href="${L.why}">only two adjacent vdevs failing</a> can then lose such a block. Metadata has <a href="${L.copies}">one more copy than <code>copies</code></a> with <code>redundant_metadata=all</code>, <a href="${L.redundant}">the default</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 5424,
                to: 5427,
                text: `The first copy starts at the class's rotor, which <a href="${L.rotor}">moves on</a> by block size, queue depth and free space (the <a href="${roomUrl(lang, "metaslabs")}">metaslabs room</a>). A class of one vdev puts every copy on it.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A replacement uses the mirror's functions</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A replacing node has a table of its own, <code>vdev_replacing_ops</code>, but the slots that move I/O hold the
          mirror's functions. Only the name in the table differs.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_replacing_ops</code>, vdev_mirror.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VM}
            from={1000}
            to={1007}
            symbol="vdev_replacing_ops"
            notes={[
              { from: 1000, to: 1001, text: "The mirror's open and close." },
              {
                from: 1006,
                to: 1007,
                text: `The mirror's <code>io_start</code> and <code>io_done</code>, as in <a href="${L.mirrorOps}"><code>vdev_mirror_ops</code></a>. The hot spare's <a href="${L.spareOps}"><code>vdev_spare_ops</code></a> fills the same slots.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The new node takes the old disk's place</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_add_parent</code> does the change to the tree. The old disk leaves its parent, the new node takes its
          place, and the old disk becomes the new node's first child.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_add_parent</code>, vdev.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={1439}
            to={1446}
            symbol="vdev_add_parent"
            notes={[
              { from: 1439, to: 1440, text: `The new node replaces the old disk among its parent's children, with <a href="${L.newId}">the same <code>vdev_id</code></a>.` },
              {
                from: 1441,
                to: 1442,
                text: `The old disk becomes child 0. <code>spa_vdev_attach</code> then <a href="${L.attachLast}">adds the new disk</a> as the last child and <a href="${L.dtl}">marks it missing</a> every txg up to now, so a read of an older block skips it until the resilver is done.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1445,
                to: 1446,
                text: `If the old disk was itself a top-level vdev, the new node becomes the top-level vdev and <a href="${L.topTransfer}">takes over</a> its metaslabs and its class.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The end of the resilver</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          After a resilver, <code>spa_vdev_resilver_done_hunt</code> looks for a replacing node whose new disk now has
          everything. It hands back the old disk to be detached.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>spa_vdev_resilver_done_hunt</code>, spa.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SP}
            from={9446}
            to={9455}
            symbol="spa_vdev_resilver_done_hunt"
            notes={[
              { from: 9449, to: 9450, text: "The newest disk is the last child, the oldest the first." },
              {
                from: 9452,
                to: 9455,
                text: `The new disk misses nothing and the old one is not needed: the old disk is detached, and the replacing node, left with one child, <a href="${L.removeParent}">is removed</a>. A spare node is <a href="${L.unspare}">handled further down</a>, and stays until the original disk is brought back or replaced, or one of the two is detached.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>dRAID: which child holds each column</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A RAID-Z group always writes to the same disks. A dRAID group of the same width can land on any of the
          children: <code>vdev_draid_map_alloc_row</code> looks each column up in a row of a table of permutations.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_draid_map_alloc_row</code>, vdev_draid.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DR}
            from={1047}
            to={1053}
            symbol="vdev_draid_map_alloc_row"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1047,
                text: `Column <code>i</code> of the group sits at position <code>(groupstart + i) mod ndisks</code> in the row. <code>ndisks</code> is the <a href="${L.derived}">children minus the distributed spares</a>.`,
              },
              { from: 1049, to: 1051, text: `A group that runs past the end of a row goes on in the next one, <a href="${L.rowHeight}">16 MiB</a> further.` },
              {
                from: 1053,
                text: `The child is the row's entry at that position, <a href="${L.getPerm}">rotated by one</a> each time the row comes round again. The table is <a href="${L.maps}">generated from a fixed seed</a> and <a href="${L.verify}">checked against a stored checksum</a>, since existing pools depend on it. The figure computes the same table.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        Which vdev of a class gets a block's first copy is decided by the allocator's rotor, by load and free space. The
        figure moves the rotor on by one vdev per block, and places each dRAID copy at the start of the next group and each
        raidz copy after the last. Block sizes are fixed: 4 KiB for the ZIL and small file blocks, 16 KiB for metadata,
        8 KiB for the deduplication table and 128 KiB for a large file block, after compression. A mirror's read takes the
        first eligible child, where the code takes the least loaded. Only the special class fills up, and its embedded
        log space stays free. Normal and special vdevs are taken to be large enough to hold an embedded log metaslab. One
        replacement per disk at a time. The resilver itself, its order and the sequential rebuild are not drawn, only its
        end. Removed (indirect) vdevs, holes, RAID-Z expansion, gang blocks and errors are left out. The 3D view draws the
        same state as the 2D one, and neither shows disk offsets.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="vdevs" />
</Base>

